@node	5.5, References, 5.4, Chapter 5
@section Compilation

The explicit-control evaluator of @ref{5.4} runs checked guest Kotlin programs
by interpreting their syntax trees.  This section studies the other execution
path in this edition: the compiler translates a checked program into typed
register-machine instructions, which the same @code{Machine} executes.

The machine is a register machine: its controller orchestrates a collection
of registers and operations.  Any computation expressible in the guest subset
can be represented by a suitable instruction sequence, although these data
paths are not necessarily convenient or efficient for every computation.
Theoretical universality does not make this particular machine a
general-purpose high-performance processor.

The guest language is the Kotlin subset defined in
@code{spec/@/host-subsets/@/kotlin/@/grammar.md}.  A checked source program is the
compiler's input; its object program is a list of typed @code{Stmt}
instructions.  The machine executes those instructions using guest values and
the compiler's registered operations.  The compiler does not lower Kotlin
syntax by rewriting it into another language, and the machine does not accept
unadmitted source.

The compiler entry points make the boundary explicit.  @code{Compiler.compile}
answers either an admission error or the instruction list.  The convenience
entry point @code{Compiler.compileAndRun} builds the compiled machine, runs it,
and answers the output, error, and main value.  Interpretation and compilation
therefore offer two ways to execute the same checked guest program.  Their
observable results are output in order, a guest error category, and the
completion value; internal stack counters need not agree.

The cross-call objective remains part of this chapter.  The interpreter and
compiler APIs do not automatically share procedure values: interpreted
closures are @code{GValue.VFunction} values, while compiled procedures are
tagged @code{GValue.VObject} records.  Exercise 5.47 asks how to bridge those
representations without weakening guest type checking or call semantics.

The compiler exposes switches for register preservation, argument order,
open-coded operators, lexical addressing, and scanning local declarations.
@code{LIVENESS} and @code{ALWAYS} preservation strategies are compared below;
@code{NEVER} deliberately demonstrates missing saves and can miscompile.
The guest contract fixes left-to-right operand evaluation, so a right-to-left
setting is not a valid alternative for effectful guest programs.

@subsubheading An overview of the compiler

The compiler and the evaluators consume the same checked syntax tree.  The
direct evaluator executes it as a guest program; the compiler traverses it at
compile time and emits typed machine instructions.  For example:

@example kotlin
fun twice(x: Long): Long = x + x

fun main() @{
    val input = 42L
    println(twice(input))
@}
@end example

@noindent
The admission checker establishes that this source belongs to the Kotlin
subset before either execution path runs it.  Compilation then specializes
the known syntax: the machine code need not rediscover at run time that
@code{twice(input)} is a call, inspect a source string, or type-check its
arguments.

Compilation also exposes opportunities to reduce machine work.  A checked
expression has a fixed tree shape, so the compiler can choose which register
values survive each subexpression.  @code{InstrSeq} records the registers a
sequence needs and modifies.  The @code{preserving} helper computes the
registers to save as
@code{(first.modifies intersect needed) - first.needs}:

@code{CompilerOptions.preserving} selects @code{LIVENESS} (the default),
@code{ALWAYS}, or @code{NEVER}; it does not choose whether the helper runs.
Valid code-generation strategies preserve guest results and effects.
@code{NEVER} and right-to-left arguments are negative controls for this
contract, as discussed at the start of the section.

The compiled machine follows a fixed register convention.  @code{env} carries
the current guest environment, @code{proc} the procedure, @code{argl} the
arguments, @code{val} the current result, and @code{continue} the return
destination.  @code{target} holds an indirect entry address.  The typed
instruction list is the object program; the compiler does not generate text
for a second parser.

The remaining subsections trace these mechanisms from the actual compiler:
typed expression dispatch, linkage, register liveness, calls, sequence
combination, lexical addressing, and the boundary between interpreted and
compiled procedures.  Exercises that compare the two execution paths use
guest Kotlin programs and preserve their explicit objectives.

