@subsection Structure of the Compiler

The compiler starts from the checked AST rather than source text.  The
admission stage has already resolved syntax and source types; code generation
then determines register operations, control-flow labels, closure entries,
and lexical environment addresses.  The result is a list of typed instructions
for the chapter 4.4 machine, not an execution procedure or a second source
program.

The public compiler accepts a checked guest program.  Compilation can still
fail while validating the program for machine generation, so the result keeps
the admission error explicit:

@example kotlin
public fun compile(
    checked: CheckedProgram,
    options: CompilerOptions = CompilerOptions(),
): Either<AdmissionError, List<Stmt>> =
    either @{
        ProgramCompiler(
            checked,
            CompileRuntime(OutputSink()),
            options,
        ).compileUnit()
    @}
@end example

The current implementation has a call-linkage defect for expression-bodied
functions: the compiler supplies the label @code{__unused__}, but emits no
label with that name.  An expression body that calls another guest function
can therefore fail during machine execution.  The examples here use block
bodies; this implementation gap remains open until the compiler assigns a
real return destination.

@noindent
The private program compiler dispatches on the checked syntax tree's typed
expression variants.  A literal or name produces a short instruction
sequence; compound expressions use dedicated generators.  Statements and
expressions are distinct AST types, so the compiler does not inspect arbitrary
lists or guess whether a value is an application:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<AdmissionError>)
private fun compileExpression(
    expression: Expression,
    linkage: Linkage,
): InstrSeq =
    when (expression) @{
        is Literal -> simple(literalStmt(expression),
            setOf("val"))
        is Name -> simple(lookupStmts(expression),
            setOf("val"))
        is Lambda -> simple(lambdaStmts(expression),
            setOf("val"))
        is Binary -> compileBinary(expression)
        is Unary -> compileUnary(expression)
        is If -> compileIf(expression, linkage)
        is Call -> compileCall(expression, linkage)
        is StringTemplate -> compileTemplate(expression)
        is Block -> compileBlock(expression)
        is Return -> compileReturn(expression)
        is When -> compileWhen(expression)
        is Member -> compileMember(expression)
        is Index -> compileIndex(expression)
        is Elvis -> compileElvis(expression)
        is Is -> simple(isStmts(expression),
            setOf("val"))
        is This ->
            simple(
                lookupStmts(Name("this",
                    expression.span)),
                setOf("val"),
            )
        is CallableReference ->
            simple(lookupStmts(Name(expression.name,
                expression.span)),
                setOf("val"))
    @}
@end example

@noindent
The register instructions are typed members of @code{Stmt}.  Their source,
condition, action, and jump targets are separate sealed types, so a label
cannot silently stand in for an operation operand.  The linker and machine
then consume the same instruction data rather than parsing rendered text.

@subsubheading Linkage and result registers

The current compiler places expression results in @code{val}; it does not
accept an arbitrary target register.  Linkage describes where control proceeds
after an expression.  The three cases are actual sealed variants:

@example kotlin
public sealed interface Linkage @{
    public data object Next : Linkage
    public data class ReturnTo(val label: String) : Linkage
    public data class BranchTo(val label: String) : Linkage
@}
@end example

@noindent
@code{Next} falls through, @code{ReturnTo} transfers to a return label, and
@code{BranchTo} transfers to a branch target.  The generated value remains in
@code{val}.  Since labels and register operands are distinct types, an
instruction cannot use a label where an operation source is required.

@subsubheading Instruction sequences and stack usage

Each code generator returns an @code{InstrSeq}: a set of registers it needs,
a set it modifies, and typed statements.  When two sequences run in order,
@code{preserving} saves a register only if the first sequence changes a value
that the second needs.  This is liveness-based stack use, not a blanket save
of every register:

@example kotlin
val produce = InstrSeq(
    needs = setOf("env"),
    modifies = setOf("val"),
    stmts = listOf(
        Assign("val", Source.RegSrc("env")),
    ),
)
val consume = InstrSeq(
    needs = setOf("val"),
    modifies = setOf("argl"),
    stmts = listOf(
        Assign("argl", Source.RegSrc("val")),
    ),
)
val combined = preserving(setOf("val"),
    produce,
    consume)
@end example

@noindent
Here @code{combined.stmts} is @code{Save("val")}, the producer, its matching
@code{Restore("val")}, and the consumer.  The metadata carries the same
contract through later combinations: needs from a sequence already modified
by an earlier sequence no longer need their original incoming value.
The data structure and liveness function are the compiler's actual public
types:

@example kotlin
public class InstrSeq(
    public val needs: Set<String>,
    public val modifies: Set<String>,
    public val stmts: List<Stmt>,
)

public fun preserving(
    needed: Set<String>,
    first: InstrSeq,
    second: InstrSeq,
): InstrSeq @{
    val mustSave = (first.modifies intersect needed) - first.needs
    val saves = mustSave.map @{ Save(it) @}
    val restores = mustSave.reversed().map @{ Restore(it) @}
    val needs = first.needs + (second.needs - first.modifies)
    return InstrSeq(
        needs,
        first.modifies + second.modifies,
        saves + first.stmts + restores + second.stmts,
    )
@}
@end example


