@subsection Compiling Expressions

The compiler's code generators consume the sealed syntax tree that admission
has already parsed and type-checked.  They emit @code{InstrSeq} values whose
statements are typed @code{Stmt} data.  No generator accepts an arbitrary
list of values as an expression.

@subsubheading Compiling literals and names

A literal becomes a compiled constant in @code{val}.  A name becomes a
compiled lookup, carrying its name and the lexical address computed from the
compiler's current frames:

@example kotlin
private fun literalStmt(literal: Literal): List<Stmt> =
    listOf(
        Assign(
            "val",
            OpSrc(
                "compiled-const",
                listOf(
                    Source.ConstSrc(literalValue(literal)),
                ),
            ),
        ),
    )

private fun lookupStmts(name: Name): List<Stmt> @{
    val found = frames.indexOfLast @{ name.text in it @}
    val depth = if (found < 0) -1 else frames.size - 1 - found
    val addressDepth =
        if (!options.lexicalAddressing) 0
        else if (depth < 0) frames.size else depth
    val displacement =
        if (depth < 0) -1 else frames[depth].indexOf(name.text)
    val address = GValue.VList(
        mutableListOf(
            GValue.VInt(addressDepth),
            GValue.VInt(displacement),
        ),
        false,
    )
    return listOf(
        Assign(
            "val",
            OpSrc(
                "compiled-lookup",
                listOf(
                    Source.ConstSrc(GValue.VString(name)),
                    Source.ConstSrc(address),
                    RegSrc("env"),
                ),
            ),
        ),
    )
@}
@end example

@noindent
The special pair of negative address components represents a lookup outside
the current compile-time frames, such as a top-level function.  Runtime
operations resolve that name in the global environment.  Local references
use the frame depth and displacement instead.

@subsubheading Compiling conditional expressions

The compiler generates a typed branch and join.  The test is a Boolean guest
value; the alternative is emitted first, then the consequent at its label:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<AdmissionError>)
private fun compileIf(
    expression: If,
    linkage: Linkage,
): InstrSeq @{
    val trueLabel = freshLabel("if-true")
    val afterLabel = freshLabel("if-after")
    val condition =
        compileExpression(expression.condition,
            Linkage.Next)
    val consequent =
        compileExpression(
            expression.yes,
            afterLinkage(linkage, afterLabel),
        )
    val alternative = expression.no?.let @{
        compileExpression(it,
                afterLinkage(linkage,
            afterLabel))
    @} ?: simple(
        listOf(Assign("val",
                    OpSrc("compiled-unit",
            emptyList()))),
        setOf("val"),
    )
    val stmts =
        condition.stmts +
            Test(OpCond("is-true",
                listOf(RegSrc("val")))) +
            Branch(trueLabel) +
            alternative.stmts +
            Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl(afterLabel)) +
            Label(trueLabel) +
            consequent.stmts +
            Label(afterLabel)
    return InstrSeq(
        condition.needs + consequent.needs + alternative.needs,
        setOf("val"),
        stmts,
    )
@}
@end example

@noindent
The accepted subset checks condition types before this stage.  A missing
alternative yields the guest unit value, and the compiler's branch sequence
preserves the selected result in @code{val}.

@subsubheading Compiling blocks and sequences

A guest block creates a child environment, compiles each statement in order,
and restores the parent environment when the block exits.  If
@code{scanOutDefines} is enabled, local functions are placed first so
mutually recursive declarations have bindings before their bodies execute:

@example kotlin
private fun compileBlock(block: Block): InstrSeq @{
    val ordered =
        if (options.scanOutDefines) @{
            block.statements.filterIsInstance<FunctionDecl>() +
                block.statements.filter @{ it !is FunctionDecl @}
        @} else @{
            block.statements
        @}
    val instructions = mutableListOf<Stmt>()
    instructions.add(Save("env"))
    instructions.add(
        Assign("env",
                OpSrc("child-env",
            listOf(RegSrc("env")))),
    )
    frames.add(mutableListOf())
    var needs = emptySet<String>()
    for (statement in ordered) @{
        if (statement is LocalProperty || statement is FunctionDecl) @{
            frames[frames.size - 1].add(declaredName(statement))
        @}
        val sequence = compileStatement(statement)
        needs = needs + sequence.needs
        instructions.addAll(sequence.stmts)
    @}
    frames.removeAt(frames.size - 1)
    instructions.add(Restore("env"))
    return InstrSeq(needs,
            setOf("val",
        "env"),
        instructions)
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Compiling lambdas

A lambda's body is emitted under a fresh entry label.  The closure value stores
that entry and the parameter names; a runtime capture operation attaches the
current guest environment:

The emitted closure is a tagged guest value, followed by a runtime capture
operation that attaches the current environment:

@example kotlin
val procedure = GValue.VObject(
    "compiled-procedure",
    false,
    mutableMapOf(
        "entry" to GValue.VString(entry),
        "params" to GValue.VList(
            params.map @{ GValue.VString(it) @}.toMutableList(),
            false,
        ),
    ),
)
val construction = listOf(
    Assign(
        "val",
        OpSrc(
            "compiled-const",
            listOf(Source.ConstSrc(procedure)),
        ),
    ),
    Assign(
        "val",
        OpSrc("compiled-capture",
                listOf(RegSrc("val"),
            RegSrc("env"))),
    ),
)
@end example

@noindent
The compiled procedure is a guest runtime object, not a host Kotlin closure.
Calls transfer to its entry with the captured environment and argument list;
the runtime preserves guest value and mutation semantics.
