@subsection Compiling Combinations

The compiler's @code{leftToRightArguments} option is intended to select
operand order, but the guest contract requires left-to-right evaluation.
Effectful programs therefore require @code{true}; the right-to-left branch is
not behavior-preserving and is treated as an implementation defect, not a
valid execution mode.  The dispatch below is from the current compiler:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<AdmissionError>)
private fun compileCall(
    expression: Call,
    linkage: Linkage,
): InstrSeq @{
    val calleeName = expression.callee as? Name
    if (calleeName != null &&
        calleeName.text in classShapes) @{
        return constructStmts(expression,
            calleeName.text)
    @}
    if (calleeName != null &&
        !resolvableName(calleeName.text)) @{
        return primitiveCallStmts(expression,
            calleeName.text)
    @}
    val memberCallee = expression.callee as? Member
    if (memberCallee != null && !memberCallee.safe) @{
        return methodCallStmts(expression, memberCallee)
    @}
    val callee = compileExpression(expression.callee,
        Linkage.Next)
    val ordered =
        if (options.leftToRightArguments) expression.arguments
        else expression.arguments.reversed()
    val argumentSeqs =
        ordered.map @{ compileExpression(it.value,
            Linkage.Next) @}
    val restoreOrder = argumentSeqs.reversed()
    val returnLabel = freshLabel("return")
    val continueLabel =
        when (linkage) @{
            is Linkage.Next -> returnLabel
            is Linkage.ReturnTo -> linkage.label
            is Linkage.BranchTo -> linkage.label
        @}
    val afterLabel = freshLabel("call-after")
    val stmts = mutableListOf<Stmt>()
    stmts.addAll(callee.stmts)
    stmts.add(Save("val"))
    for (argument in argumentSeqs) @{
        stmts.addAll(argument.stmts)
        stmts.add(Save("val"))
    @}
    stmts.add(Assign("argl",
                OpSrc("compiled-empty-args",
        emptyList())))
    repeat(restoreOrder.size) @{
        stmts.add(Restore("val"))
        stmts.add(
            Assign(
                "argl",
                OpSrc(
                    "adjoin-arg",
                    listOf(RegSrc("val"),
                        RegSrc("argl")),
                ),
            ),
        )
    @}
    stmts.add(Restore("proc"))
    stmts.add(Assign("continue",
        LabelSrc(continueLabel)))
    stmts.add(
        Assign(
            "target",
            OpSrc("procedure-entry",
                listOf(RegSrc("proc"))),
        ),
    )
    stmts.add(Goto(GotoTarget.ByReg("target")))
    stmts.add(Label(afterLabel))
    if (linkage is Linkage.Next) stmts.add(Label(returnLabel))
    val needs =
        callee.needs + argumentSeqs.flatMap @{ it.needs @}.toSet()
    return InstrSeq(
        needs,
        setOf("val",
            "argl",
            "proc",
            "continue",
            "target",
            "env"),
        stmts,
    )
@}
@end example

@noindent
Named data-class constructors and primitive calls take specialized paths;
ordinary function values use the general procedure path.  A method call
evaluates and saves its receiver before evaluating its arguments.  Each path
keeps the call's operand order explicit rather than relying on host evaluation
order.

The argument builder saves each computed value, then restores them in reverse
stack order while adding them to the guest argument list.  The helper used by
the general path is:

@example kotlin
private fun argumentSave(
    argumentSeqs: List<InstrSeq>,
    stmts: MutableList<Stmt>,
) @{
    for (argument in argumentSeqs) @{
        stmts.addAll(argument.stmts)
        stmts.add(Save("val"))
    @}
    stmts.add(Assign("argl",
                OpSrc("compiled-empty-args",
        emptyList())))
    repeat(argumentSeqs.size) @{
        stmts.add(Restore("val"))
        stmts.add(
            Assign(
                "argl",
                OpSrc(
                    "adjoin-arg",
                    listOf(RegSrc("val"),
                        RegSrc("argl")),
                ),
            ),
        )
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Primitive operands use the same ordering option.  Open-coded binary
operations can bypass generic procedure dispatch; other primitives are
looked up by name and applied through the runtime operation table.  The
@code{openCodedPrimitives} option selects this optimization:

@example kotlin
private fun primitiveApplyStmts(
    name: String,
    arity: Int,
): List<Stmt> @{
    if (arity != 2) @{
        return listOf(
            Assign(
                "val",
                OpSrc(
                    "compiled-primitive",
                    listOf(
                        Source.ConstSrc(GValue.VString(name)),
                        RegSrc("argl"),
                    ),
                ),
            ),
        )
    @}
    return listOf(
        Assign(
            "argl",
            OpSrc(
                "compiled-pair-args",
                listOf(RegSrc("val"), RegSrc("argl")),
            ),
        ),
        Assign(
            "val",
            OpSrc(
                "compiled-primitive",
                listOf(
                    Source.ConstSrc(GValue.VString(name)),
                    RegSrc("argl"),
                ),
            ),
        ),
    )
@}
@end example

@noindent
Compiled calls place their entry in @code{target} and jump through that
register.  The current @code{compileReturn} compiles its value with
@code{Linkage.Next} and then emits a jump through @code{continue}; it does
not directly pass the caller's return destination through a tail call.
Consequently, guest tail position alone is not evidence of constant machine
stack use.  @ref{Exercise 5.34} asks the reader to inspect the generated
instructions and measured stack behavior.
