@subsection Combining Instruction Sequences

An @code{InstrSeq} carries three things together: incoming register needs,
modified registers, and typed machine statements.  Sequential composition
combines their metadata at the same time that it appends their instructions.
If the first sequence writes a register, the second sequence no longer needs
the register's incoming value:

@example kotlin
public fun appendSeq(
    first: InstrSeq,
    second: InstrSeq,
): InstrSeq =
    InstrSeq(
        first.needs + (second.needs - first.modifies),
        first.modifies + second.modifies,
        first.stmts + second.stmts,
    )
@end example

@noindent
The set difference in @code{needs} is the important part.  It prevents a
later read from becoming a spurious requirement on the sequence's initial
register state after an earlier instruction has assigned that register.
@code{preserving}, shown in @ref{5.5.1}, uses the same metadata to decide
whether a save/restore pair is required.

Code for a compiled procedure body is present in the instruction list but is
not executed while the procedure value is created.  @code{tackOnInstrSeq}
therefore appends statements without folding the body sequence's register
needs and modifications into the construction path:

@example kotlin
public fun tackOnInstrSeq(
    seq: InstrSeq,
    stmts: List<Stmt>,
): InstrSeq =
    InstrSeq(seq.needs, seq.modifies, seq.stmts + stmts)
@end example

@noindent
Alternative branches are different from sequential composition.  The
conditional generator emits a test, one branch label, and two instruction
paths, and conservatively combines the needs of both paths.  It does not use
the sequential set difference between alternatives.  The distinction is
structural: only one path executes, but either path can require an incoming
register value.


