@subsection An Example of Compiled Code

The compiler accepts checked guest source, not raw text or untyped reader
values.  This complete Kotlin-subset program defines recursive factorial:

@example kotlin
val factorialSource =
    """
    fun factorial(n: Long): Long @{
        if (n == 1L) return 1L
        return n * factorial(n - 1L)
    @}

    fun main() @{
        println(factorial(5L))
    @}
    """.trimIndent()
@end example

@noindent
Admission parses and type-checks the source before compilation.  The public
compiler returns typed instructions; the convenience entry point compiles and
runs the same checked program:

@example kotlin
val checked = Admission.admit(factorialSource,
    Mode.CORE).fold(
    @{ error -> error("factorial source did not admit: $error") @},
    @{ it @},
)
val instructions = Compiler.compile(checked).fold(
    @{ error -> error("factorial source did not compile: $error") @},
    @{ it @},
)
val run = Compiler.compileAndRun(checked)
@end example

@noindent
The instruction list is the object program.  Its typed labels, assignments,
tests, branches, saves, restores, and gotos are consumed directly by the
register machine.  No renderer output is re-parsed as instructions.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Figure 5.17}Figure 5.17:} The factorial compilation path:
guest source, admission, typed code generation, and machine execution.
