@node	5.5.6, 5.5.7, 5.5.5, 5.5
@subsection Lexical Addressing

The compiler tracks names in compile-time frames so that a local reference can
be associated with the same nested environment frame that will exist at run
time.  Consider a guest closure that reads both a captured value and its
parameter:

@example kotlin
fun main() @{
    val x = 3L
    val f: (Long) -> Long = @{ a ->
        val y = a + x
        y
    @}
    println(f(4L))
@}
@end example

@noindent
When the compiler handles the body that reads @code{a}, @code{x}, and
@code{y}, the compile-time frames grow from outermost to innermost.  The local
@code{x} and @code{f} share one block frame, the lambda parameter has its own
frame, and the lambda body block adds another:

@example
outermost to innermost:
[]
[]
[x, f]
[a]
[y]
@end example

@noindent
At the read of @code{y}, the intended addresses are @code{y = (0, 0)},
@code{a = (1, 0)}, and @code{x = (2, 0)}.  The first component is the number
of parent frames; the second is the position within that frame.  The
compiler's @code{lookupStmts} encodes both components with the name and current
environment.

There is an important implementation gap in the current runtime:
@code{compiledLookup} reads the frame depth but resolves the name by searching
the selected frame; it ignores the encoded displacement.  Likewise,
@code{compiledAssign} performs a name search rather than a displacement-based
write.  The compiler therefore emits a lexical-address pair, but execution
does not yet provide direct indexed lookup and assignment.  The
@code{lexicalAddressing = false} path also needs verification against this
contract.  The exercises retain the required address semantics rather than
claiming the implementation already meets them.

The frame list itself is compile-time data: it contains names, not values.
The runtime environment contains the corresponding cells and their values.
Bindings outside the compile-time frames, including global names, require the
runtime's global resolution path.

@quotation
