@node	5.5.7, References, 5.4, 5.5
@subsection Interfacing Compiled Code to the Evaluator

The edition admits and type-checks a guest program once, then offers distinct
execution paths over that checked program.  Direct evaluation, explicit
control, and compilation share the guest source contract:

@example kotlin
val checked = Admission.admit(factorialSource,
    Mode.CORE).fold(
    @{ error -> error("source did not admit: $error") @},
    @{ it @},
)
val direct = Direct.run(checked)
val controlled = ExplicitControl.run(checked)
val compiled = Compiler.compileAndRun(checked)
@end example

@noindent
The results can be compared for guest output, error category, and main value.
Stack and instruction counters are observations of one engine; they are not
cross-engine correctness criteria.

This interface does not make procedure values interchangeable.  The direct
and explicit-control evaluators represent closures as
@code{GValue.VFunction}; the compiler constructs tagged
@code{GValue.VObject} values for compiled procedures.  The current public
entry points run a checked program in one engine at a time.  They do not
provide a general adapter for calls across interpreted and compiled code.

The distinction matters when porting a language or comparing execution
strategies.  Interpretation executes the checked syntax in terms of guest
operations.  Compilation emits controller instructions specialized to that
syntax.  Both must preserve the same guest errors and effects, but their
internal instruction and stack counts are not the guest behavior contract.
Checks for unassigned reads, index bounds, and other guest errors remain part
of the runtime; speed does not justify silently replacing a guest error with a
host failure.

Safety checks have a cost, so a compiler should avoid checks already proved
unnecessary and keep the behavior required by the guest contract.  A host
compiler may also expose deliberately unsafe experiments, such as the
@code{NEVER} preservation mode of @ref{Exercise 5.37}, but it must not present
them as semantics-preserving.  The historical buffer-overflow example remains
relevant: the @abbr{UNIX}(tm) finger daemon failure exploited a missing input
buffer check; see @ref{Spafford 1989}.  The point is to measure safe code
generation, not to remove required checks from the guest runtime.

Stack allocation and garbage collection remain competing runtime strategies;
see @ref{Hanson 1990}, @ref{Appel 1987}, and @ref{Miller and Rozas 1994}.

The same two strategies suggest different implementation paths for another
host.  One can implement the explicit-control machine and its guest runtime
on the target, or lower checked programs through the compiler and provide the
operations and storage that emitted code requires.  The C backend in this
edition emits an artifact string; it is not guest source and is compiled only
by the documented native toolchain.  Exercise 5.50 also tests the stronger
case: the compiled program is itself a guest evaluator.

